\title{Dependent function types}
\import{prelude}

\p{Coln is a dependently typed language; this means that the definition of a type is permitted to refer to elements of other types. In fact, we have seen this a lot already without remarking on it, in that later fields of a record are permitted to refer to earlier ones when describing their type. From [[001D]], we had:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} TourWithGuests := \kw{sig}
  Child : \kwi{Set}
  Adult : \kwi{Set}
  charlie : Child
  grandpa-joe : Adult
\kw{end}
}

\p{This is already a dependent record: the \em{type} of the field \code{charlie} refers to the \em{element} \code{Child}, which only exists because we declared it to previously.}

\p{Besides dependent records, Coln also includes dependent functions; these are functions where the type of the output is permitted to depend on the element that the function is provided as input. The syntax for these is \code{(x : A) -> B}, where \code{B} allowed to mention \code{x}.}

\p{There are especially useful when dealing with families of types:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} TourOutcomes := \kw{sig}
  Departure : \kwi{Set}
  Child : Departure -> \kwi{Set}

  ironic-comeuppance : (d : Departure) -> Child d -> \kwi{String}
\kw{end}
}

\dbexample{
  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.Departure}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{Row ID}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{d1}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{d2}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.Child}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{Row ID} \hcell{\code{a : root.Departure}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{c1}} \cell{\code{d1}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{c2}} \cell{\code{d1}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{c3}} \cell{\code{d2}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.ironic-comeuppance}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{d : root.Departure}} \hcell{\code{a : root.Child}} \hcell{\code{b : \kwi{String}}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{d1}} \cell{\code{c1}} \cell{\code{"blueberry inflation"}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{d1}} \cell{\code{c2}} \cell{\code{"down the rubbish chute"}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{d2}} \cell{\code{c3}} \cell{\code{"taffy-pulling"}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Constraints:}}
  \ul{
    \li{Each value in column \code{a} of \code{root.Child} is a row ID in \code{root.Departure}.}
    \li{Each row of \code{root.ironic-comeuppance} pairs a departure in column \code{d} with a child belonging to that departure in column \code{a}.}
    \li{Every pair of a departure and a child belonging to it appears exactly once in \code{root.ironic-comeuppance}.}
  }
}

\p{Here, we wish to store an outcome for each child on each departure. Again this is stored as a three-column table. The improvement over a non-dependent function is that the constraints ensure that each entry of the \code{Child} column is correctly paired with the \code{Departure} element that it belongs to.}

\p{We are not restricted to simply using variables when constructing a dependent type. Here's an example where we use the first argument as an input to a function, selecting one \code{\kwi{Set}} in an indexed family:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} BoatRideSong := \kw{sig}
  Stop : \kwi{Set}
  Hazard : Stop -> \kwi{Set}
  next : Stop -> Stop

  ominous-stanza : (s : Stop) -> Hazard (next s) -> \kwi{String}
\kw{end}
}

\dbexample{
  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.Stop}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{Row ID}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{s1}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{s2}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{s3}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.Hazard}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{Row ID} \hcell{\code{a : root.Stop}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{h1}} \cell{\code{s2}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{h2}} \cell{\code{s2}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.next}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{a : root.Stop}} \hcell{\code{b : root.Stop}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{s1}} \cell{\code{s2}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{s2}} \cell{\code{s3}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{s3}} \cell{\code{s3}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.ominous-stanza}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{s : root.Stop}} \hcell{\code{a : root.Hazard}} \hcell{\code{b : \kwi{String}}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{s1}} \cell{\code{h1}} \cell{\code{"There's no earthly way of knowing..."}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{s1}} \cell{\code{h2}} \cell{\code{"Which direction we are going..."}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Constraints:}}
  \ul{
    \li{Each value in column \code{a} of \code{root.Hazard} is a row ID in \code{root.Stop}.}
    \li{Each value in columns \code{a} and \code{b} of \code{root.next} is a row ID in \code{root.Stop}.}
    \li{Every row ID in \code{root.Stop} appears exactly once in column \code{a} of \code{root.next}.}
    \li{Each row of \code{root.ominous-stanza} pairs a stop in column \code{s} with a hazard at its next stop in column \code{a}.}
    \li{Every pair of a stop and a hazard at its next stop appears exactly once in \code{root.ominous-stanza}.}
  }
}

\p{Here this gives, for every stop and every hazard that is associated with the \em{next} stop, a string.}
